  </div><div class="case-card" style="margin-bottom:24px;">
    <div class="case-header"><div class="case-badge">Case 3</div><div class="case-title">Susan — Chronic Back Pain &amp; MRI Beliefs</div></div>
    <div class="case-body">
      <div style="margin-bottom:12px;"><span style="display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:4px;background:var(--green-pale);color:var(--green);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;padding:3px 9px;border-radius:12px;margin:2px 3px 2px 0;">Age: <span style="font-weight:400;">43</span></span><span style="display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:4px;background:var(--green-pale);color:var(--green);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;padding:3px 9px;border-radius:12px;margin:2px 3px 2px 0;">Presentation: <span style="font-weight:400;">Chronic LBP + avoidance</span></span><span style="display:inline-flex;align-items:center;gap:4px;background:var(--green-pale);color:var(--green);font-size:10.5px;font-weight:600;padding:3px 9px;border-radius:12px;margin:2px 3px 2px 0;">Workshop Focus: <span style="font-weight:400;">Sandstone misconception · MI + conceptual change</span></span></div>
      <p class="case-scenario">Susan is a 43-year-old woman with chronic lower back pain. She brings an MRI report showing a disc bulge at L4, which she firmly believes is the structural root cause of all her pain. She hurt her back at the gym and hasn't returned since. She would love to hike with her husband again. She is cautious, avoidant, and her confidence is low. Multiple previous clinicians have framed her condition in structural terms.</p>
      <div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr);gap:10px;margin:16px 0;"><div style="background:#FEF2F2;border:1px solid #FCA5A5;border-radius:var(--r-sm);padding:14px;"><div style="font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:var(--navy);letter-spacing:0.07em;text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:8px;">⚠️ Misconception Indicators</div><ul style="list-style:none;padding:0;"><li style="padding:2px 0;font-size:12px;color:var(--body);">"Disc bulge = root cause of all pain"</li><li style="padding:2px 0;font-size:12px;color:var(--body);">Exercise caused injury to worsen (faulty attribution)</li><li style="padding:2px 0;font-size:12px;color:var(--body);">Multiple clinicians have reinforced this view</li><li style="padding:2px 0;font-size:12px;color:var(--body);">MRI as "proof" — sandstone territory</li></ul></div><div style="background:#EFF6FF;border:1px solid #BFCBFE;border-radius:var(--r-sm);padding:14px;"><div style="font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:var(--navy);letter-spacing:0.07em;text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:8px;">🔄 Behaviour Change Profile</div><ul style="list-style:none;padding:0;"><li style="padding:2px 0;font-size:12px;color:var(--body);">Contemplation — wants to change, fears the attempt</li><li style="padding:2px 0;font-size:12px;color:var(--body);">Confidence very low (2–3/10)</li><li style="padding:2px 0;font-size:12px;color:var(--body);">Change talk anchor: hiking with husband</li></ul></div><div style="background:var(--gold-pale);border:1px solid #E8C070;border-radius:var(--r-sm);padding:14px;"><div style="font-size:11px;font-weight:700;color:var(--navy);letter-spacing:0.07em;text-transform:uppercase;margin-bottom:8px;">🎯 Goals</div><ul style="list-style:none;padding:0;"><li style="padding:2px 0;font-size:12px;color:var(--body);">Return to hiking with husband</li><li style="padding:2px 0;font-size:12px;color:var(--body);">Be less limited by pain day-to-day</li><li style="padding:2px 0;font-size:12px;color:var(--body);">Reduced fear of movement</li></ul></div></div>
      
      <div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 280px;gap:16px;margin-top:16px;">
      <div style="background:var(--green-xpale);border-radius:var(--r-sm);padding:18px 20px;">
        <div style="font-size:10.5px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:0.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--green);margin-bottom:12px;">Discussion Questions</div>
        <ol style="padding-left:18px;"><li style="padding:5px 0;font-size:13px;color:var(--body);line-height:1.55;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,0.05);">What type of misconception does Susan hold, and how entrenched is it?</li><li style="padding:5px 0;font-size:13px;color:var(--body);line-height:1.55;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,0.05);">What are the key "grains" to address, and in what order?</li><li style="padding:5px 0;font-size:13px;color:var(--body);line-height:1.55;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,0.05);">How would you use holistic confrontation with Susan?</li><li style="padding:5px 0;font-size:13px;color:var(--body);line-height:1.55;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,0.05);">What change talk points connect to Susan's stated values?</li><li style="padding:5px 0;font-size:13px;color:var(--body);line-height:1.55;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,0.05);">Write a brief "nugget" metaphor to gently challenge Susan's structural beliefs.</li><li style="padding:5px 0;font-size:13px;color:var(--body);line-height:1.55;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,0.05);">How would scaling questions guide your first session?</li><li style="padding:5px 0;font-size:13px;color:var(--body);line-height:1.55;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(0,0,0,0.05);">What risks exist if the OT directly confronts Susan's MRI beliefs without using MI first?</li></ol>
      </div>
      <div>
        <div style="background:var(--navy);border-radius:var(--r-sm);padding:14px;margin-bottom:10px;">
          <div style="font-size:10px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:0.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--gold);margin-bottom:8px;">Facilitator Focus</div>
          <div style="display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:2px;"><span style="background:var(--navy);color:rgba(255,255,255,0.8);font-size:10.5px;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:10px;margin:2px;">Sandstone misconception</span><span style="background:var(--navy);color:rgba(255,255,255,0.8);font-size:10.5px;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:10px;margin:2px;">Holistic confrontation</span><span style="background:var(--navy);color:rgba(255,255,255,0.8);font-size:10.5px;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:10px;margin:2px;">Change talk</span><span style="background:var(--navy);color:rgba(255,255,255,0.8);font-size:10.5px;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:10px;margin:2px;">Nuggets</span><span style="background:var(--navy);color:rgba(255,255,255,0.8);font-size:10.5px;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:10px;margin:2px;">Expectation violation</span></div>
        </div>
        <div style="background:var(--gold-pale);border:1px solid #E8C070;border-radius:var(--r-sm);padding:14px;">
          <div style="font-size:10px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:0.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#7A5010;margin-bottom:8px;">💡 Facilitator Insight</div>
          <ul style="list-style:none;padding:0;"><li style="padding:2px 0;font-size:12px;color:var(--body);">→ Confronting the MRI belief directly will create resistance — MI must come first.</li><li style="padding:2px 0;font-size:12px;color:var(--body);">→ Her relationship with her husband is the strongest change talk anchor — start there.</li><li style="padding:2px 0;font-size:12px;color:var(--body);">→ A nugget: "The brain can turn up the volume on pain signals even when structural issues are stable."</li></ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    </div>
  </div><div class="takeaways-box"><h3>Key Takeaways</h3><div class="takeaways-grid"><div class="takeaway-item">MI is collaborative — the clinician draws out the client's own motivation, not their own</div><div class="takeaway-item">Always identify the clinician's primary task before choosing an approach</div><div class="takeaway-item">OARS, RULE and Change Talk are core conversation tools used together</div><div class="takeaway-item">Misconception type determines the appropriate addressing strategy</div><div class="takeaway-item">Match your approach to the client's stage — pushing action before readiness creates resistance</div><div class="takeaway-item">Deep conceptual change — not surface compliance — is what drives lasting outcomes</div><div class="takeaway-item">Behaviour change laws: make it obvious, easy, enjoyable, and satisfying</div><div class="takeaway-item">The client's own words and reasoning carry far more weight than the clinician's</div></div></div>
  </div>
